The core difference between HTTP and HTTPS in APIs lies in security: HTTPS is the secure version of HTTP that uses encryption to protect data transmitted between a client and a server, while HTTP sends data in plain text [1, 2]

When you make an API call over HTTPS, the interaction is protected by an SSL/TLS certificate [2, 3]:
Handshake: The client and server perform a "handshake" to verify each other's certificates and agree on encryption methods [3].
Encryption: Once the connection is established, all data exchanged (including sensitive information like API keys, user credentials, and request/response bodies) is encrypted [3]. This means that even if a third party intercepts the data, they cannot read it

